<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benue River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Benue is the main tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, about 1,400 kilometres long, rising in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> and joining the Niger at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokoja">Lokoja</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Navigable for much of its course in the rainy season, it was a corridor for trade and migration, and the Jukun kingdom of Kwararafa, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of Adamawa and Muri all lay along it. British traders and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-niger-company">Royal Niger Company</a> used it to reach the interior in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benue-river.jpg" alt="Benue River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benue_10.38241E_8.69279N.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
